00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:15.880
On excess returns, market strategist Jim Paulson breaks down weak earnings breadth beneath the headline numbers, with tech and telecom doing much of the lifting.

38
00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:25.080
That matters when broad monetary policy is supporting an economy whose sectors are moving in very different directions.

39
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:33.640
But below that surface, it's really a uh uh two-prong, almost three-pronged story on earnings here.

40
00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.480
And that's what this chart brings out.

41
00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:51.520
What I got here is forward 12-month earnings per share for the blue line is for the technology and telecommunication sectors, kind of new era, which I call the brainiac earnings, you know, earnings that come out from somebody who really came up with something cool.

42
00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.240
Um, and they're just going gay busters, which everyone knows.

43
00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:01.600
Um, and then there's another part that doesn't get as much attention but should, and that's the green line.

44
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:07.120
And that is those that are tied to energy commodity prices, basically, or commodity prices in general.

45
00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:08.800
And they're not a big part.

46
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.160
The the blue line's about not quite 50% of the market cap.

47
00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.600
The the green line's only 5%, but it's up unbelievably over this period of time, and it's mainly up.

48
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.320
We all know the reason because oil prices went up so much.

49
00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:20.960
Okay.

50
00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:24.800
And that's uh that's what I call the no-brainer earnings.

51
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:31.680
Because if you're an oil company, you're gonna make money even if you're a terrible manager, they're gonna go up like the green lines going up.

52
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:42.400
But there's this red line down there, which is all the rest, it's the remaining seven sectors of the SP, market cap weighted and so forth, and they're barely up this year.

53
00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.800
If it was just those seven sectors, no one would be saying earnings are great.

54
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.280
Because they're up like six percent annualized year to date.

55
00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:55.920
If you black back out inflation of three and a half to four, we're talking about two and a half percent real earnings growth.

56
00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:01.360
No one would be celebrating that as some kind of overwhelming positive force.

57
00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:06.720
And but if you look at SP overall, you average it, hey, we're doing great.

58
00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:07.840
And that's what I'm saying.

59
00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:20.080
I think I think we really need to separate these stories because if if I'm a policy official and I could do it, I'd be tightening on the blue and I'd be easing like a banjoe on the red.

60
00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:22.400
Problem is we got no policies like that.

61
00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.480
Not aggregate macro policies.

62
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.680
So uh we have to choose later next week, you know, whether we're gonna tighten or ease.

63
00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:45.720
And I think there's too much calm, is I guess what I'm getting at, too much average analysis, which leads people to believe that you know a strong index can hide a much narrower earnings story.

64
00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:56.600
On Masters of Scale, Axio's CEO Jim Vandehee describes the widening gap between aggressive AI adopters and companies trapped in the messy middle.

65
00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:03.240
With bots stripping traffic from publishers almost overnight, this isn't a distant productivity debate.

66
00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:09.080
It's already reshaping distribution and revenue.

67
00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:10.920
Well, that's a lot of people.

68
00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:17.400
You're talking about millions of people that were customers or coming to and tasting our content that no longer are.

69
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.320
And that happened almost overnight last couple of months.

70
00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:24.760
Huge surge in the number of bots that are attacking websites.

71
00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:33.000
There are these machines that are kind of hitting your site trying to get information, trying to get data, and they're kind of polluting the open web.

72
00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.040
And that's new.

73
00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:36.040
Okay, well, let's wrestle with that.

74
00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:46.680
I think that's why there is a twilight zone feeling right now, where people are like they're hearing about this technology that could end humanity and it could you could start a business with one person.

75
00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.880
And most people are like, I don't know, I don't really use it.

76
00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.360
Or if I use it, it's kind of a glorified search engine.

77
00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:53.960
Like, what's all the fuss about?

78
00:06:53.960 --> 00:07:03.160
And I think the demarcation zone is people in companies that use it persistently and aggressively live in a different world right now than those that don't.

79
00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:08.720
So if you go out to Silicon Valley, they feel like, oh my God, this is like 1999.

80
00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.120
Like the world is changing.

81
00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:11.400
I'm on the cutting edge.

82
00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.080
Everything's about to explode.

83
00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:19.280
But then you go to Toledo or you go to Topeka or you go to Oshkosh, and most people are like, I don't know, I'm just running my company, man.

84
00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.600
I guess there's this new AI, and uh my employees are talking about it.

85
00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.160
I'm sure we'll get to that one day soon.

86
00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:29.600
And I think that's somewhat natural because it's a new technology.

87
00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:41.760
Uh, I think introducing any kind of technology into an existing company is hard, because even if it's the coolest technology ever, you have to hook it up to all your clunky systems that you built over the last decade, and that's necessarily messy.

88
00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.120
I call it the messy middle, which is where I think most companies are right now.

89
00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:53.440
They're starting to use AI, they're prototyping, they're seeing the promise, and then they're realizing, oh crap, I gotta hook this up to my data reservoir.

90
00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:53.680
Right.

91
00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:56.560
This is a lot harder than I than I thought it was gonna be, right?

92
00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:58.240
This is way harder than I thought.

93
00:07:58.240 --> 00:07:58.640
Yeah.

94
00:07:58.640 --> 00:07:59.040
Yeah.

95
00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:05.120
So simplify opens with this this bracing line that AI isn't simplifying your life.

96
00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:08.640
It's mass-producing more options, more decisions, more noise.

97
00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:14.800
I I know I find myself sometimes falling into a rabbit hole as I explore new things with AI.

98
00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:21.680
For media companies, waiting for clarity can mean losing the audience before the strategy is ready.

99
00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:34.080
On the art of manliness, the conversation turns to AI as a complexity creation machine, multiplying presentations, emails, context, and demands on attention.

100
00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:42.960
A useful counterpoint to today's platform announcements: more capability doesn't automatically mean less work.

101
00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:49.120
How are you seeing AI increase the amount of complexity in our lives?

102
00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:53.680
Because the idea, the selling point is AI is supposed to simplify our lives.

103
00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.920
I think with time, AI might simplify our lives.

104
00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:03.400
I think in the short term, it is the greatest complexity creation machine ever created.

105
00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:05.240
And let me let me say why.

106
00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:07.240
Because think about it at work.

107
00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:16.840
Like, yes, if you really know how to use it with great expertise, and you're one of the one percent where it's a true force multiplier, it probably saves you time.

108
00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:23.240
What is actually happening on the ground is now everybody can do better presentations, everybody can write more detailed memos.

109
00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:24.600
So everybody is.

110
00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:37.320
So suddenly we are all getting lit up with more presentations, with more emails, with more context, with more ideas at a time where we have less time to actually focus on it.

111
00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:50.840
So unless you're really careful and you think about this almost like a system or an operating system or a mindset, you're just gonna end up in a bigger complexity trap than you were already caught in pre-AI.

112
00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:52.760
Yeah, I've seen it in my own work.

113
00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:58.040
So we get a lot of PR emails from people who want to be on the podcast.

114
00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:05.480
And I'd say in the past year, the amount of those emails that we've gotten have increased probably 10x.

115
00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:06.760
And I think it's AI.

116
00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:07.720
It's all AI.

117
00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:13.240
And again, like that's that's one example across one small part of your workday.

118
00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:17.640
For most of us, we're getting hit with that from a hundred different directions.

119
00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:22.920
You add that up and you end up wasting so much time.

120
00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:27.480
Like the reason we wrote this book, I am not somebody, I think we talked about this a couple years ago.

121
00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:31.800
I have no sort of egotistical scratch to itch in terms of writing books.

122
00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:37.800
You know, I've reached a point in life, I'm 55, I've had some successes, I've won the lottery with my family.

123
00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:42.280
So, like I'm playing with house money, and I want to like spend my time.

124
00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:44.440
Like, how do you be useful to others?

125
00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:46.240
And I think this book is useful.

126
00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:50.480
I think about it with content, I think about it with time allocation.

127
00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:47.600
I love that word useful.

128
00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:56.960
When producing becomes nearly free, filtering becomes the real job.

129
00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:01.920
